00:51:26,510 --> 00:51:35,364
That's one of those that's like very magical. Like you just want to set it in one place, and you don't want you just have to remember to import it and remember to put it in there.

586
00:51:35,694 --> 00:51:42,020
But then there's the other approach where it's like, okay, you might create middleware for doing common tasks and operations.

587
00:51:42,382 --> 00:51:51,808
So within endpoints that manage things for Teams, you might want to include a workspace and Teams middleware that you just don't have to think about,

588
00:51:51,968 --> 00:51:55,620
right? But the workspace can just always provide you with workspace context.

589
00:51:56,142 --> 00:52:00,260
The team can always provide you with team context and it can handle permissions and all that stuff.

590
00:52:00,686 --> 00:52:04,932
You need to be able to compose those things in at the site of the server function.

591
00:52:05,326 --> 00:52:06,500
So you kind of have to have both.

592
00:52:06,862 --> 00:52:16,288
You have to have the primitive of composition, but you also have to have the abstraction of automatic wizardry so that you know each type of developer,

593
00:52:16,448 --> 00:52:25,248
be it super junior early dev, can do his little functional composition, and staff engineer can make sure he's enforcing things across the board.

594
00:52:25,582 --> 00:52:28,644
But that's the idea for middleware is that we're going to do all of it.

595
00:52:29,008 --> 00:52:30,212
And middleware,

596
00:52:30,990 --> 00:52:38,048
commonly you'll see it split up into uh you have a next function passed to you, you await it, you get the result, and then you do something with it.

597
00:52:38,128 --> 00:52:42,928
So very common to see kind of the next function approach.

598
00:52:43,168 --> 00:52:50,276
But to do to do inferred type safety well, you need to be able to have a return type that you can infer.

599
00:52:50,638 --> 00:52:54,020
You can't infer what some next function is called with.

600
00:52:54,446 --> 00:52:58,848
So what we do is we split that phase up and we say we have a before and we have an after.

601
00:52:59,022 --> 00:53:04,260
Before is everything that you would do before you call next, and what you return will be the arguments into next.

602
00:53:04,528 --> 00:53:09,764
And then after, you'll have everything that you would do after next, including the result and anything that you returned.

603
00:53:10,158 --> 00:53:18,208
That way we can infer the types for all of the middlewares going down the chain and compose them together and make sure that they are all compatible.

